PURPLETUTORIAL — Purplecode + Purplebootcode
===========================================

Purplecode is for userland programs. Purplebootcode (Purplebc) is a separate
freestanding language profile for kernel/boot code. The syntax looks familiar,
but its API surface deliberately excludes Linux, libc, files, GUI, network and
other userland functions.

BUILD
-----
  make clean all
  make test
  ./purplebc examples/boot_hello.purplebc -o kernel.elf
  ./purplebc examples/boot_hello.purplebc --format bin -o kernel.bin
  readelf -h kernel.elf
  file kernel.bin

ELF output is a freestanding 32-bit i386 kernel image linked at 1 MiB with a
Multiboot2 header. A Multiboot2-capable bootloader (such as GRUB) is needed to
enter protected mode and load it. The raw .bin output is the flat image of the
linked kernel payload; it is not a 512-byte BIOS boot sector and cannot be
written to sector zero and expected to boot on its own. With the default linker
script, the entry offset in the flat binary is the ELF entry address minus
0x100000; a custom loader must also initialize the omitted BSS region.

LANGUAGE
--------
  fn main() { ... }                    required entry function
  let x: int = 123                    32-bit integer local
  let p: ptr = Memory.Alloc(256)      kernel heap allocation (1 MiB heap)
  Memory.Write32(p, 0x50555250)
  let x: int = Memory.Read32(p)
  Memory.Copy(dst, src, bytes)
  Memory.Set(dst, 0, bytes)
  let used: int = Memory.HeapUsed()
  let free: int = Memory.HeapFree()
  Memory.Free(p)                     free/coalesce a heap block
  Screen.Clear()
  Screen.Write("Hello kernel!\n")    VGA text-mode output
  Port.Out8(0xE9, 65)                 x86 port I/O
  let v: int = Port.In8(0x60)
  Disk.ReadSector(lba, buffer)        ATA PIO 28-bit LBA read (512 bytes)
  Disk.WriteSector(lba, buffer)       ATA PIO 28-bit LBA write (512 bytes)
  CD.ReadSector2048(lba, buffer)      ATAPI primary-master read (2048 bytes)
  ISO9660.FileSize(path)              ISO9660 file size, or -1 on error
  ISO9660.ReadFile(path, buffer, cap) read file bytes into caller buffer
  Screen.WriteBuffer(buffer)          write a NUL-terminated buffer to VGA text
  let cr0: int = CPU.ReadCR0()
  CPU.WriteCR3(page_directory)
  CPU.Invlpg(address)
  CPU.CLI(); CPU.STI(); CPU.Halt()
  System.Panic("fatal kernel error")

Available low-level built-ins: Memory.Alloc/Free, Memory.Read8/16/32/64,
Memory.Write8/16/32/64, Memory.Copy/Set/HeapUsed/HeapFree, Port.In8/In16/In32,
Port.Out8/Out16/Out32, CPU.CLI/STI/Halt/ReadFlags/ReadCR0/WriteCR0/ReadCR3/
WriteCR3/Invlpg, Screen.Clear/Write/WriteBuffer, Disk.ReadSector/WriteSector,
CD.ReadSector2048, ISO9660.FileSize/ReadFile and System.Panic. The initial heap is a 1 MiB coalescing allocator. Port I/O, CR0/CR3
writes, and raw disk writes are privileged and can crash the machine or destroy
data if used incorrectly. Test in an emulator/VM with a disposable disk image.

USERLAND CHECKING
-----------------
  ./purplebc --help
  ./purplebc --check source.purplebc
  ./purplesdk check source.purplebc
  ./purplesdk build source.purplebc -o kernel.elf
  ./purplesdk bin source.purplebc -o kernel.bin

Calls such as Data.ShellPrint, FS.Read, Web.*, Image.*, GUI.*, Kernel.Call,
System.Process.*, CD.*, USB.* and Program.ELF64.* are rejected with:
ERROR: ATTEMPTED TO USE USERLAND FUNCTION IN BOOT CODE
Offending API: <function name>
REWRITE IT TO NOT USE USERLAND FUNCTIONS

PURPLE LIBS
-----------
A Purple Lib may be imported with #uselib "library.pl". Purplebc expands its
source body, then applies the same boot-code API checks. Library functions must
be freestanding and must not depend on Purplecode's userland runtime.

LINKING
-------
  purplelinker.ld is the default linker script. Override it with
  --linker-script path/to/purplelinker.ld. ELF is linked directly as the final
  output; the compiler does not leave a .s file. Internally, this implementation
  uses GCC's freestanding native-code backend and GNU ld.

IMAGE / ISO PACKAGING
--------------------
  ./tools/purpleimage.py --img system.pbfimg --kernel kernel.elf \
      --inject config.txt:/etc/purple/config.txt --inject logo.bin:/assets/logo.bin

PBFIMG1 is a custom fixed-size payload container with a manifest, kernel and
injected files. It is NOT a standard partitioned disk image and is not directly
BIOS bootable. For a bootable Multiboot2 ISO, install grub-mkrescue and xorriso:
  ./tools/purpleimage.py --iso system.iso --kernel kernel.elf \
      --inject config.txt:/boot/config.txt

The ISO uses GRUB's multiboot2 loader to start the ELF kernel. Injected files
are placed in the ISO filesystem. A read-only ISO9660 primary-volume driver is
available through ATAPI on the primary-master optical drive. Example:
  ./purplebc examples/boot_iso_read.purplebc -o kernel.elf
  ./tools/purpleimage.py --iso system.iso --kernel kernel.elf \
      --inject config.txt:/BOOT/CONFIG.TXT
The driver does not yet support Joliet/Rock Ridge, USB optical devices, or general
block-device discovery.

UEFI ISO packaging (hybrid BIOS/UEFI GRUB image) requires x86_64 EFI GRUB modules:
  ./tools/purpleimage.py --iso system.iso --uefi --kernel kernel.elf \
      --inject config.txt:/BOOT/CONFIG.TXT
This boots via GRUB EFI, not a native `.efi` Purplebc kernel; test the i386 kernel
with your firmware/emulator before deployment.

PURPLECODE VS PURPLEBC
----------------------
Purplecode compiles programs for a hosted OS and provides a runtime. Purplebc
is freestanding i386 kernel code and uses no libc, Linux syscalls or Purplecode
runtime. It currently targets Multiboot2 i386 protected-mode kernels, not UEFI
applications or 16-bit BIOS boot sectors. Use a real emulator/VM to test kernels;
never experiment with raw disk writes on your everyday system drive.
